      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Cervicocogenic headache (CeH) is a relatively common disorder. Although on ideal treatment is available so far, blockades in different structures and nerves may be temporarily effective. We studied the effects of 1-2 mL 0.5% bupivacaine injection at the ipsilateral greater occipital nerve (GON) in 41 CeH patients. The pain is significantly reduced both immediately and as long as 7 days after the blockade. The improvement is less marked during the first two days, a phenomenon we called \"tilde pattern\". GON blockades may reduce the pool of exaggerated sensory input and antagonize a putative \"wind-up-like effect\" which may explain the headache improvement."},
